{{Short description|2009 fashion collection by Alexander McQueen}}
{{good article}}
{{Italic title}} {{Use dmy dates|date=April 2025}} {{Use British English|date=April 2025}}

[[File:McQueen, Musée des beaux-arts - 39.jpg|thumb|Digitally-printed minidresses from the collection presented at the ''Alexander McQueen: Mind, Mythos, Muse'' exhibition at [[Musée national des beaux-arts du Québec]], 2023]]

'''''Natural Dis-tinction Un-natural Selection''''' is the thirty-third collection by British fashion designer [[Alexander McQueen]], released for the Spring/Summer 2009 season of his [[Alexander McQueen (brand)|eponymous fashion house]]. The collection reflected McQueen's concerns about [[human impact on the environment]], while drawing inspiration from the work of naturalist [[Charles Darwin]]. ''Natural Dis-tinction'' is known for its pioneering use of digital prints, which were engineered to match the [[Pattern (sewing)|patterns]] of their garments.     

Two distinct phases of designs contrasted the beauty of the environment with the impact of human industry. The first phase used soft colours, organic motifs, and flowing lines to present an image of nature unspoiled by man. The second featured prints manipulated to look unnaturally perfect, with some designs in artificially bright colours and others in black and white. Across both phases, McQueen presented both classical and experimental silhouettes. Forty-one looks were presented at the collection's [[runway show]], which was staged on 3 October 2008 at the [[Cent Quatre]] building in Paris, a community centre that was once a [[mortuary]]. The catwalk was framed by two lines of exotic [[taxidermy]] animals; a large round screen at the rear of the stage showed projections of celestial bodies and McQueen's own eyeball.      

Contemporary critical response was somewhat mixed, with journalists preferring the more romantic first half of the show. Some were disappointed at the lack of runway theatrics for which McQueen had been known. McQueen continued to explore environmental themes and digital prints in his following two collections; he is credited with popularising the use of such prints in fashion. Ensembles from ''Natural Dis-tinction'' are held by various museums and have appeared in exhibitions such as the McQueen retrospective ''[[Alexander McQueen: Savage Beauty]]''.

== Background ==
British fashion designer [[Alexander McQueen]] was known for his imaginative, sometimes controversial designs, and dramatic [[Fashion show|fashion shows]].<ref name=":1723">{{Cite web |title=Alexander McQueen – an introduction |url=https://www.vam.ac.uk/articles/alexander-mcqueen-an-introduction |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210317231608/https://www.vam.ac.uk/articles/alexander-mcqueen-an-introduction |archive-date=17 March 2021 |access-date=25 June 2024 |website=[[Victoria and Albert Museum]]}}</ref>{{sfn|Mora|Berry|2022|pp=126, 128, 132}} During his nearly twenty-year career, he explored a broad range of ideas and themes, including [[Historicism (art)|historicism]], [[romanticism]], femininity, sexuality, and death.<ref name=":1723" />{{sfn|Mora|Berry|2022|pp=126, 128, 132}}{{Sfn|Bolton|2011|pp=13–14}} He learned tailoring as an apprentice on [[Savile Row]], and dressmaking as head designer at French fashion house [[Givenchy]].{{efn|From 1996 to October 2001, McQueen was simultaneously head designer at [[Givenchy]] and at his own label.{{Sfn|Wilcox|2015|p=327}}{{Sfn|Wilson|2015|p=255}}}}<ref name=":322">{{Cite web |last=Bowles |first=Hamish |author-link=Hamish Bowles |date=15 July 2010 |title=Alexander McQueen: Noble farewell |url=https://www.vogue.com/article/alexander-mcqueen-noble-farewell |access-date=30 January 2025 |website=[[Vogue (magazine)|Vogue]]}}</ref> Although he worked in [[ready-to-wear]] – clothing produced for retail sale – his showpiece designs featured a degree of craftsmanship that verged on ''[[haute couture]]''.<ref name=":422">{{Cite web |last=Socha |first=Miles |date=4 November 2002 |title=King McQueen: A mature Alexander McQueen cast aside the shock values (and even the adolescent rants) to produce a dazzling spring collection. (Movers Shakers) |url=https://go.gale.com/ps/i.do?p=ITOF&u=wikipedia&id=GALE%7CA94237973&v=2.1&it=r&sid=bookmark-ITOF&asid=6d861079 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230601053346/https://go.gale.com/ps/i.do?p=ITOF&u=wikipedia&id=GALE%7CA94237973&v=2.1&it=r&sid=bookmark-ITOF&asid=6d861079 |archive-date=1 June 2023 |access-date=31 May 2023 |website=[[Women's Wear Daily]]}}</ref><ref name="vogue22">{{Cite web |last=Mower |first=Sarah |date=9 March 2010 |title=Alexander McQueen Fall 2010 Ready-to-Wear Collection |url=https://www.vogue.com/fashion-shows/fall-2010-ready-to-wear/alexander-mcqueen |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210417204015/https://www.vogue.com/fashion-shows/fall-2010-ready-to-wear/alexander-mcqueen |archive-date=17 April 2021 |access-date=17 January 2025 |website=[[Vogue (magazine)|Vogue]]}}</ref><ref name="menkes2">{{Cite news |last=Menkes |first=Suzy |author-link=Suzy Menkes |date=9 March 2010 |title=McQueen's mesmerizing finale |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2010/03/10/fashion/10iht-rmcq.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250114225802/https://www.nytimes.com/2010/03/10/fashion/10iht-rmcq.html |archive-date=14 January 2025 |access-date=6 February 2025 |work=[[The International Herald Tribune]] |issn=0362-4331}}</ref>

McQueen's personal fixations had a strong influence on his work. He incorporated his love of animals and the environment throughout his career by using organic materials and making visual references to nature.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Alexander McQueen – an introduction |url=https://www.vam.ac.uk/articles/alexander-mcqueen-an-introduction |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210317231608/https://www.vam.ac.uk/articles/alexander-mcqueen-an-introduction |archive-date=17 March 2021 |access-date=17 January 2023 |website=[[Victoria and Albert Museum]]}}</ref>{{Sfn|Watt|2012|p=252}} He was fascinated with [[taxidermy]], and owned several pieces, including stuffed [[alligator]] heads and a whole [[polar bear]].{{Sfn|Skogh|2015|p=180–183}}{{Sfn|Wilson|2015|p=316}} Preserved animals and animal parts had appeared in many previous collections: ''[[It's a Jungle Out There (collection)|It's a Jungle Out There]]'' (Autumn/Winter 1997) had jackets with horns and crocodile heads, while ''[[Voss (collection)|Voss]]'' (Spring/Summer 2001) featured a dress made with taxidermied hawks positioned as though attacking the model.{{Sfn|Skogh|2015|p=180–183}}{{Sfn|Wilson|2015|p=196}}{{Sfn|Bethune|2015|p=312}} 

In the final years of his career, McQueen became increasingly concerned about [[Human impact on the environment|human activity damaging the natural world]].{{Sfn|Watt|2012|p=252}}{{Sfn|Gleason|2012|p=187}}{{Sfn|Bethune|2015|p=320}} He felt that humans were beginning to overpower nature and were "in danger of killing the planet through greed", which would lead to the [[extinction]] of life on earth.<ref name=":4">{{Cite news |last=Cartner-Morley |first=Jess |author-link=Jess Cartner-Morley |date=3 March 2008 |title=Exotic creatures: McQueen's bid to save planet |url=https://www.theguardian.com/lifeandstyle/2008/oct/04/catwalk.parisfashionweek |access-date=24 April 2025 |work=[[The Guardian]]}}</ref><ref name=":3">{{Cite news |last=Frankel |first=Susannah |date=6 April 2009 |title=Natural beauty |url=https://link.gale.com/apps/doc/A197201960/STND?u=wikipedia&sid=bookmark-STND&xid=e21da685 |access-date=24 April 2025 |work=[[The Independent]]}}</ref> Variations on these ideas formed the basis for his last three completed collections.{{Sfn|Watt|2012|p=252}}{{Sfn|Gleason|2012|p=187}}{{Sfn|Bethune|2015|p=320}}

== Concept and collection ==
[[File:Lee Alexander McQueen & Ann Ray - Rendez-Vous 15.jpg|thumb|Look 7 at ''Lee Alexander McQueen & Ann Ray - Rendez-Vous'' (2024)]]
''Natural Dis-tinction Un-natural Selection'' (Spring/Summer 2009) is the thirty-third collection McQueen made for his eponymous [[Alexander McQueen (brand)|fashion house]].{{Sfn|Fox|2012|p=138}} According to the show's program notes, McQueen drew inspiration from the [[theory of natural selection]] as described by [[Charles Darwin]] in his seminal 1859 work ''[[On the Origin of Species]]''.{{Sfn|Gleason|2012|p=187}}{{Sfn|Wilson|2015|p=317}} ''Natural Dis-tinction'' is known for its pioneering use of digital prints, which were engineered to match the [[Pattern (sewing)|patterns]] of their garments, rather than the usual practice of the pattern being cut to accommodate an existing print.{{sfn|Esguerra|Hansen|2022|p=89}}{{Sfn|Knox|2010|p=97}} The collection's prints were derived from natural materials, in the latter half manipulated to appear [[Kaleidoscope|kaleidoscopic]].{{Sfn|Fairer|Wilcox|2016|p=276}}{{sfn|Esguerra|Hansen|2022|p=119}} Two distinct phases of designs contrasted the beauty of nature with the impact of human industry.{{Sfn|Watt|2012|p=252}}<ref name=":1">{{Cite web |last=Mower |first=Sarah |date=3 October 2008 |title=Alexander McQueen Spring 2009 Ready-to-Wear Collection |url=https://www.vogue.com/fashion-shows/spring-2009-ready-to-wear/alexander-mcqueen |access-date=11 October 2024 |website=[[Vogue (magazine)|Vogue]]}}</ref>{{Sfn|Bethune|2015|p=320}}

The first portion of the collection visually referenced unspoiled nature using soft colours, natural materials like leather, delicate embroidery, loose [[Fringe (trim)|fringe]], and flowing, organic shapes.{{Sfn|Gleason|2012|p=187}}{{Sfn|Bethune|2015|p=320}}{{Sfn|Watt|2012|pp=253–255}} Prints in this section were floral or derived from [[wood grain]] in light shades of brown.<ref name=":1" />{{Sfn|Watt|2012|pp=253–255}} Many designs featured embroidered floral motifs or fabric flowers.{{Sfn|Bethune|2015|p=320}}{{Sfn|Fairer|Wilcox|2016|p=276}}{{Sfn|Gleason|2012|p=191}} In some, the flowers were set beneath transparent [[Tulle (netting)|tulle]], evoking imagery of scientific specimens kept beneath glass, as was common in the Victorian era.{{Sfn|Bethune|2015|p=320}} The skull, by that point a McQueen signature, appeared on the front of a white minidress in Look 7.{{Sfn|Gleason|2012|p=189}}

In the second half of the collection, inspiration shifted to the industrial and modern, with two contrasting colour schemes. Some designs had patterns in artificially bright colours, while the rest were in shades of black and white. The prints in this section were still mainly derived from nature – flowers, crystals, and bones – but were digitally manipulated to look unnaturally angular and symmetrical.{{Sfn|Bethune|2015|p=320}}{{Sfn|Watt|2012|pp=253–254}}{{Sfn|Homer|2023|p=112}} McQueen described the prints as organic in nature but "enhanced, synthetic - touched by man".<ref name=":3" /> Other prints were based on buildings like the [[Eiffel Tower]] or a photograph by [[Dan Holdsworth]].{{Sfn|Bethune|2015|p=320}}{{Sfn|Watt|2012|pp=253–254}}{{Sfn|Homer|2023|p=112}} Artificial and structured textiles predominated: [[Lycra]] for leggings and dresses, and [[bonded leather]] dyed black, calling to mind [[fetish wear]].{{Sfn|Bethune|2015|p=320}}{{Sfn|Watt|2012|pp=253–255}} Some dresses and jumpsuits were encrusted with minerals; the final look was a jumpsuit covered with pieces of real [[amber]].{{Sfn|Wilson|2015|p=317}}{{Sfn|Fairer|Wilcox|2016|p=276}}{{Sfn|Gleason|2012|p=192}}

Across the collection, the silhouettes were both classical and experimental. The garments were designed to fit an antique [[Victorian era]] mannequin, leading to what [[Susannah Frankel]] called a "slightly truncated hourglass silhouette".<ref name=":3" /> Wearable items like [[leggings]] and [[Shift dress|shift dresses]] hinted at McQueen's efforts to keep the collection viable for retail sales.<ref name=":1" />{{Sfn|Knox|2010|p=96}} Despite this, McQueen's characteristic tailoring appeared in precisely-cut minidresses, [[Frock coat|frock coats]], and slim [[Trouser suit|trouser suits]].<ref name=":1" />{{Sfn|Knox|2010|p=100}} High [[Edwardian era|Edwardian]] collars and [[Leg-of-mutton sleeve|leg-of-mutton sleeves]] showcased his ever-present [[Historicism (art)|historicist]] tendencies, as did designs that appeared to reference ''The Tailor's Pattern Book'' (1589) by Spanish mathematician [[Juan de Alcega]].{{Sfn|Gleason|2012|p=189}}{{Sfn|Honigman|2021|p=54}}{{sfn|Bethune|2015|p=313}} 

McQueen had become known for playing with the silhouette by cutting or structuring garments to produce unusual shapes, and he returned to these ideas in ''Natural Dis-tinction''.{{Sfn|Watt|2012|p=|pp=212, 253}} The collection's more experimental designs exaggerated the wearer's body beyond natural human proportions, with cinched waists and padded shoulders and hips.{{Sfn|Gleason|2012|p=189}}{{Sfn|Knox|2010|p=100}}{{sfn|Wilcox|2015|p=89|ref={{sfnref|Wilcox in Wilcox}}}} Intricately folded fabric created coccoon dresses that surrounded the body. Other short dresses had structured, rounded skirts resembling [[bell jar]]s.{{sfn|Wilcox|2015|p=89|ref={{sfnref|Wilcox in Wilcox}}}}{{Sfn|Wilson|2015|p=317}}{{Sfn|Fairer|Wilcox|2016|p=276}} 

== Runway show ==

=== Staging and design ===
[[File:Alexander McQueen - Natural Dis-tinction Un-natural Selection SS 2009 - Look 24.jpg|thumb|Look 24 as presented at ''Fashion Fictions'' at the [[Vancouver Art Gallery]] (2023)]]
The runway show was staged on 3 October 2008 at the [[Cent Quatre]] building in Paris, a community centre that was once a [[mortuary]].{{Sfn|Fairer|Wilcox|2016|p=347}}{{Sfn|Wilson|2015|p=317}} The invitation featured a [[Lenticular printing|lenticular]] portrait of McQueen that transformed into a skull. McQueen's nephew, Gary McQueen, who worked as a menswear assistant with the company, had created the skull image as a print for the menswear line.{{Sfn|Thomas|2015|p=346}}{{Sfn|O'Neill|2015|p=274}} McQueen decided he wanted to use it instead and had his nephew rework it into the shifting image for the invite.{{Sfn|O'Neill|2015|p=274}}

McQueen typically worked with a consistent creative team for his shows. Overall styling was handled by [[Tabitha Simmons]], while Gainsbury & Whiting were responsible for production.{{Sfn|Fairer|Wilcox|2016|p=347}} Joseph Bennett, who had designed all of McQueen's runways since [[No. 13 (Alexander McQueen collection)|''No. 13'']] (Spring/Summer 1999), returned for [[set design]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=16 March 2015 |title=Interview: Joseph Bennett on Lee McQueen |url=https://www.showstudio.com/projects/unseen_mcqueen/interview_joseph_bennett?autoplay=1 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240215021422/https://www.showstudio.com/projects/unseen_mcqueen/interview_joseph_bennett?autoplay=1 |archive-date=15 February 2024 |access-date=14 February 2024 |website=[[SHOWstudio]]}}</ref><ref name="tt" /> Hair was styled by Paul Hanlon, make-up by [[Peter Philips (make-up artist)|Peter Philips]].{{Sfn|Fairer|Wilcox|2016|p=347}} 

The catwalk was framed by two lines of exotic [[taxidermy]] animals, including an elephant, giraffe, and several large cats.{{Sfn|Gleason|2012|p=187}} Accordingly, the show opened with a soundtrack of animal noises – roaring, hoofbeats, and elephant calls – before transitioning to [[electronic dance music]].{{Sfn|Wilson|2015|p=317}}{{Sfn|Gleason|2012|p=|pp=189, 192}} A large round screen at the rear of the stage featured various projections of the Earth, the moon, and the sun, and McQueen's own eyeball.{{Sfn|Wilson|2015|p=317}}{{Sfn|Bethune|2015|p=320}}<ref name="tt">{{Cite web |title=Day 4: Joseph Bennett |url=https://www.showstudio.com/projects/tumblr-takeover-joseph-bennett/day-4 |url-status= |access-date=22 March 2025 |website=[[SHOWstudio]]}}</ref> McQueen's typical runway theatrics were absent.{{Sfn|Gleason|2012|p=192}}

=== Show ===
Models were styled with see-through hairnets used as veils over their heads and faces.{{Sfn|Fox|2012|p=138}}{{Sfn|Gleason|2012|p=189}} Author Chloe Fox felt they provided a "foetal sameness" as though the models were freshly-born specimens of a "new breed".{{Sfn|Fox|2012|p=138}}

Forty-one looks were presented in roughly two phases.<ref name=":1" />{{Sfn|Watt|2012|p=253}} The first half of the collection featured designs that represented the natural world before human interference. These looks featured lighter colours, floral elements, and prints of wood grain. The phase closed with a trio of minidresses covered with metallic enamel flowers, contrasting the softness of the preceding items.{{Sfn|Bethune|2015|p=320}}  

The second half of the show opened with a short series of tailored minidresses in brightly-coloured patterns before transitioning to eveningwear, with leather dresses and tailored patterned looks in monochrome.{{Sfn|Watt|2012|pp=253–254}}{{Sfn|Homer|2023|p=112}} The final four looks were a series of dresses and jumpsuits encrusted with minerals, including the last jumpsuit, covered with pieces of real [[amber]].<ref name=":1" />{{Sfn|Bethune|2015|p=320}}{{Sfn|Gleason|2012|p=192}} After the models took their final turn, McQueen came out to take his bow in a rabbit [[costume]].{{Sfn|Wilson|2015|p=317}}{{Sfn|Fairer|Wilcox|2016|p=276}}

== Reception ==
[[File:Lee Alexander McQueen & Ann Ray - Rendez-Vous 21.jpg|thumb|Look 30 at ''Rendez-Vous'']]
Critical reception for ''Natural Dis-tinction'' was somewhat mixed. Anne Slowey of [[Elle (magazine)|''Elle'']] reported that critical reception for ''Natural Dis-tinction'' was "lukewarm", but felt the industry was "charmed" by his appearance in a rabbit suit.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Slowey |first=Anne |date=11 March 2010 |title=Angels and demons: Remembering Alexander McQueen |url=https://www.elle.com/fashion/a10959/angels-demons-alexander-mcqueen-435631/ |access-date=28 April 2025 |website=[[Elle (magazine)|Elle]]}}</ref>

The environmentalist theme attracted commentary from journalists. Sarah Mower of [[Vogue (magazine)|''Vogue'']] and the staff writer for [[Women's Wear Daily|''Women's Wear Daily'']] (''WWD'') were surprised that it did not lead to the kind of dark, angry collection that McQueen was known for.<ref name=":1" /><ref name=":5" /> [[Jess Cartner-Morley]] of ''[[The Guardian]]'' thought the theme was ironic given the use of animal materials in McQueen's designs.<ref name=":4" /> In an April 2009 article for [[The Independent|''The Independent'']], Frankel considered ''Natural Dis-tinction'' the most fully-realised exploration of an environmental theme that ran through the entire Spring/Summer 2009 season. She called the collection proof that conceptual fashion no longer needed to "denote heaviness or obvious difficulty", and could still be light and commercially viable.<ref name=":3" /> 

Critics generally preferred the first half of the collection.<ref name=":4" /><ref name=":1" /><ref name=":5" /> The ''WWD'' writer felt the first half was "more beautiful" than the latter.<ref name=":5" /> Mower was pleased with the romantic execution and highlighted the fringed designs as "two of the season's most sublime".<ref name=":1" /> Cartner-Morley felt the best designs were those which balanced "pretty and menacing" elements.<ref name=":4" /> Her favourite was Look 2, a minidress which featured yellow silk flowers beneath layers of see-through mesh.<ref name=":4" />

Several critics noted the adherence to McQueen's typical tailored shapes.<ref name=":4" /><ref name=":1" /><ref name=":5">{{Cite web |last= |first= |date=3 October 2008 |title=Alexander McQueen RTW Spring |url=https://wwd.com/fashion-news/shows-reviews/alexander-mcqueen-rtw-spring-1586291/ |access-date=28 April 2025 |website=[[Women's Wear Daily]]}}</ref> Frankel felt McQueen's workmanship was improving each season, citing his embroidery, fabric choices, and digital prints.<ref name=":6">{{Cite news |last=Frankel |first=Susannah |author-link=Susannah Frankel |date=3 October 2008 |title=McQueen weaves some animal magic in a show of natural delight |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/life-style/fashion/news/mcqueen-weaves-some-animal-magic-in-a-show-of-natural-delight-950866.html |access-date=27 April 2025 |work=[[The Independent]]}}</ref> Mower thought McQueen's choice to produce a colourful collection with "eased-up tailoring" was intended to promote sales; she felt it would be commercially viable.<ref name=":1" /> 

Look 16, a brown bodysuit with long patterned coat, attracted commentary for its unusual, inhuman-looking design. [[Claire Wilcox]] wrote that it "stretched the thorax of the model, suggesting genetic mutation", while Katherine Gleason described the wearer as appearing like "a visiting dignitary from another planet".{{Sfn|Gleason|2012|p=191}}{{Sfn|Fairer|Wilcox|2016|p=276}} Both [[Andrew Wilson (author)|Andrew Wilson]] and Judith Watt described the pointed shoulders of the coat as resembling a [[manta ray]].{{Sfn|Wilson|2015|p=317}}{{Sfn|Watt|2012|p=254}}

Some reviewers were disappointed with the lack of runway performance art, which they had come to expect from McQueen.{{Sfn|Gleason|2012|p=192}} Despite this, the ''WWD'' reviewer considered the show a strong one with a "stunning" set and called it "among the most intriguing in a lackluster season".<ref name=":5" />

Despite the mixed contemporary reception, authors have retrospectively considered the collection's ideas intriguing. Wilson felt the clothes presented "the look of an alternative species, something that might have stepped from a space-age [[Noah's Ark|Noah’s Ark]]."{{Sfn|Wilson|2015|p=317}} Chloe Fox wrote that the "thoroughly modern looks cemented McQueen’s reputation as a sartorial soothsayer, a man with a passionate commitment to the evolution of fashion".{{Sfn|Fox|2012|p=138}}

== Analysis ==
[[File:Lee Alexander McQueen & Ann Ray - Rendez-Vous 33.jpg|thumb|Look 18, one of the bell jar dresses, at ''Rendez-Vous'']]Wilson took the rabbit costume as a possible reference to [[Donnie Darko|''Donnie Darko'']] (2001), in which a man in a rabbit suit predicts the end of the world.{{Sfn|Wilson|2015|p=317}} Watt likened it to the genetically-engineered monster rabbit that causes chaos in the animated film [[Wallace & Gromit: The Curse of the Were-Rabbit|''Wallace & Gromit: The Curse of the Were-Rabbit'']] (2005).{{Sfn|Watt|2012|p=255}} 

According to O'Neill, McQueen claimed that the skull design was based on the desiccated head of Mrs. Bates seen in the film [[Psycho (1960 film)|''Psycho'']] (1960), although he notes that this contradicts the actual origin of the image.{{Efn|McQueen was a [[cinemaphile]] who frequently referenced his favourite films in his work.{{Sfn|O'Neill|2015|p=262}}}} O'Neill suggested that the ''Psycho'' reference indicated that McQueen was marking the collection as being of an "unnatural order", given the perverse posthumous relationship between Mrs. Bates and her son [[Norman Bates]].{{Sfn|O'Neill|2015|p=274}} In an essay about McQueen's use of [[Symbols of death|death symbolism]], Eleanor Townsend cited the skull image from the ''Natural Dis-tinction'' invitation as an example of the designer's awareness of his own mortality.{{sfn|Townsend|2015|p=159}}

Fashion journalist Alex Fury argued that ''Natural Dis-tinction'' was an example of McQueen expressing his vision "through the bodies of his models" in conjunction with the stripped-down set dressing. The clothing, with its exploration of evolution and nature, is contrasted with the backdrop of the taxidermy animals.{{Sfn|Fury|2015|pp=226}} Researcher Lisa Skogh noted that McQueen often incorporated concepts and objects which might have appeared in a [[cabinet of curiosities]] – collections of natural and historical objects that were the precursor to modern museum.{{Sfn|Skogh|2015|p=179}} She identified the taxidermy animals lining the runway as part of this tradition, likening them to "a re-created [[Garden of Eden]]".{{Sfn|Skogh|2015|p=180–182}}

== Legacy ==
McQueen continued to explore the impact of humanity on nature in his next two collections. ''[[The Horn of Plenty]]'' (Autumn/Winter 2009) concerned wastefulness in the fashion industry and ''[[Plato's Atlantis]]'' (Spring/Summer 2010), his final completed collection, envisioned a flooded post-[[climate change]] world.{{Sfn|Watt|2012|p=252}} Considering the collections in retrospect, Wilcox wrote that ''Natural Dis-tinction'' clearly "foreshadowed" its "exceptionally innovative" successor, ''Plato's Atlantis'', which had similar dresses with colourful, digitally-worked patterns.{{sfn|Wilcox|2015|p=89|ref={{sfnref|Wilcox in Wilcox}}}}

''Natural Dis-tinction'' prompted a trend for printed [[Lycra]] garments such as leggings, which reappeared in his next collections and were imitated by other brands.{{Sfn|Knox|2010|p=98}} McQueen is credited with popularising the use of digitally-engineered prints in fashion, beginning with this collection.{{Sfn|Watt|2012|p=253}}''{{Sfn|Fox|2012|p=138}}{{sfn|Esguerra|Hansen|2022|p=89}}'' He continued to experiment with this novel technique in his remaining collections.{{Sfn|Frankel|2011|p=27}}

=== Media and celebrity wear ===
[[File:Camilla Belle 2009.jpg|thumb|Actress [[Camilla Belle]] wearing Look 24]]
Actress Cate Blanchett wore a version of Look 39, one of the crystal-covered bell jar dresses, to the Los Angeles premier of the film [[The Curious Case of Benjamin Button (film)|''The Curious Case of Benjamin Button'']] (2008).<ref name=":0">{{Cite news |date=8 March 2015 |title=''Alexander McQueen: Savage Beauty'' |url=https://www.theguardian.com/fashion/gallery/2015/mar/08/alexander-mcqueen-savage-beauty |access-date=22 March 2025 |work=[[The Guardian]]}}</ref> Actress [[Camilla Belle]] wore Look 24 to the Los Angeles premier of [[Push (2009 film)|''Push'']] (2009).<ref>{{Cite web |last= |first= |date=2 February 2009 |title=Red-carpet flash: Camilla Belle |url=https://wwd.com/feature/red-carpet-flash-camilla-belle-1961668-1554294/ |access-date=26 April 2025 |website=[[Women's Wear Daily]]}}</ref> ''[[InStyle]]'' magazine selected it as one of their 100 best dresses of the 2000s, saying the "extravagant" print was "incredibly flattering".<ref>{{Cite web |last=Rubenstein |first=Hal |date=2009 |title=100 best dresses of the decade |url=http://www.instyle.com/instyle/package/general/photos/0%2C%2C20322779_20327331_20711436%2C00.html |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20091222095230/http://www.instyle.com/instyle/package/general/photos/0,,20322779_20327331_20711436,00.html |archive-date=22 December 2009 |access-date=26 April 2025 |website=[[InStyle]]}}</ref>

Several looks from ''Natural Dis-tinction'' have been photographed for ''Vogue''. Lachlan Bailey photographed Look 13, while [[Nick Knight (photographer)|Nick Knight]], who worked with McQueen often, photographed one of the skeleton-printed suits.{{Sfn|Fox|2012|pp=5, 159}} [[Mario Testino]] photographed an editorial featuring a houndstooth skirt suit in 2009. Pop singer [[Lady Gaga]] wore the one-shoulder black mermaid gown from Look 43 for a shoot by Josh Olins. [[Patrick Demarchelier]] and [[Tim Walker]] photographed Look 10 and Look 2, respectively.{{Sfn|Fox|2012|pp=18, 139–141, 159}}

=== Museum ownership and exhibitions ===
A pair of shoes from ''Natural Dis-tinction'' appeared in ''[[Alexander McQueen: Savage Beauty]]'', a retrospective exhibition of McQueen's designs originally staged at the [[Metropolitan Museum of Art]] in 2011. They were placed in the Cabinet of Curiosities section with other accessories.{{Sfn|Bolton|2011|p=235}} The image from the ''Natural Dis-tinction'' invitation, with McQueen's face shifting into a skull, was used for the front cover of the [[exhibition catalogue]].{{Sfn|Wilson|2015|p=317}}

Three items owned by the [[Los Angeles County Museum of Art]] appeared in the museum's exhibition ''Lee Alexander McQueen: Mind, Mythos, Muse'', originally staged in 2022: Looks 20 and 32, digitally-printed [[Minidress|minidresses]], and a pair of shoes.{{Sfn|Esguerra|Hansen|2022|p=172}} 

When the [[National Gallery of Victoria]] (NGV) in Australia restaged ''Mind, Mythos, Muse'', they added garments from their own collection: Look 12, a beige printed jacket and skirt set; Look 15, a fringed dress; and Look 22, a blue printed jacket and leggings set.{{refn|<ref>{{Cite web |title=Look 15, dress |url=https://www.ngv.vic.gov.au/explore/collection/work/142165/ |archive-url= |archive-date= |access-date=21 March 2025 |website=[[National Gallery of Victoria]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Look 22, jacket and leggings |url=https://www.ngv.vic.gov.au/explore/collection/work/149349/ |archive-url= |archive-date= |access-date=21 March 2025 |website=[[National Gallery of Victoria]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Jacket |url=https://www.ngv.vic.gov.au/explore/collection/work/147950/ |archive-url= |archive-date= |access-date=21 March 2025 |website=[[National Gallery of Victoria]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Skirt |url=https://www.ngv.vic.gov.au/explore/collection/work/148011/ |archive-url= |archive-date= |access-date=21 March 2025 |website=[[National Gallery of Victoria]]}}</ref>}}

